] [17592186193610 #edb/ref 1061 41046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193610 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4242fb45-13fd-57f1-834d-c5d8bf8b6731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193610 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193719 #edb/ref 1061 466026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c89b0360-91da-5424-aedb-da76a815447b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193720 #edb/ref 1061 466027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0da282af-995a-5faf-b0d0-876baec229e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193721 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193721 #edb/ref 1061 466031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cde1c192-24a5-5dc6-a1eb-22b02b4431e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193722 #edb/ref 1061 466034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "44d32822-3dee-5d1f-bc85-937a6b36e438" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193826 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to compute inverses of reciprocal functions.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the function $f(x)$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{7}{2x+4}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{7}{2x+4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{7}{2y+4}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{7}{2y + 4} \\\\\nx(2y + 4) &= 7 \\\\\n2xy + 4x &= 7 \\\\\n2xy &= 7 - 4x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{7 - 4x}{2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{7 - 4x}{2x}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193826 #edb/ref 1065 6512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193826 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1be3fc12-b770-5a76-ab56-1385a320d85f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193826 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193861 #edb/ref 1064 "When drawing angles expressed in radians, it can be helpful to convert the angle to degrees first. This is especially helpful when writing trigonometric ratios in terms of reference angles, as we usually need to determine which quadrant the angle lies in.\n\nLet's express the following trigonometric ratio in terms of a reference angle:\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nYou might find it tricky to visualize this angle straight off the bat. So, let's start by converting the angle measure into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = 120^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $120^\\circ$, which lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/34/342602449228f93042121b22f39d6cc203a6d945934a9ddb726e862838832815.png)\n\nNow, to express\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nin terms of $\\sin\\theta_R$, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R$.\n2. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R$.\n3. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= π - θ \\\\\n&= π - \\frac{2π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3π}{3} - \\frac{2π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\n, and therefore we're interested in\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta_R = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nmust be *positive* because the sine ratio is *always* positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193861 #edb/ref 1065 6765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193861 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4923e69b-5a86-59ae-ab93-5cd5e68e7ef0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193861 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198350 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198350 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198350 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198350 #edb/ref 1039 "Eliminating the First Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198350 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198350 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "772da46a-8b9c-54e7-8661-a5865b1df4e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198351 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198351 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198351 #edb/ref 1039 "Eliminating the Second Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198351 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198351 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9cf591e2-45d5-580c-9d46-c6a8645c0513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198352 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198352 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198352 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198352 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the First Variable"