 17592186213859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213858 #edb/ref 1061 478498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213858 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "501a0f87-638b-5be9-988b-a3d9207c5314" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213858 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186213017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213859 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186213860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213859 #edb/ref 1061 478501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213859 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "494c1398-1801-5014-8bc0-39d88af8010b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213859 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186213018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213860 #edb/ref 1061 478504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213860 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb9fd774-ee90-5072-becc-85b80612fb62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213860 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186213019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213927 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186213928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213927 #edb/ref 1061 485545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213927 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3cac7a4f-be5f-5268-8b3d-21f275556a87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213927 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186214233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213928 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186213929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213928 #edb/ref 1061 485546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213928 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b04c002-e8c7-54a8-8375-43fcf5243adc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213928 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186213066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213929 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186213930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213929 #edb/ref 1061 485549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213929 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54f29bf8-3b84-513a-a72e-97f67a67acbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213929 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186213067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213930 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186213931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213930 #edb/ref 1061 485553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213930 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "08e9ff75-663e-532d-b5f9-2d4420b6160f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213930 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186214234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213931 #edb/ref 1061 485554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213931 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "656e8d0e-9a81-5393-bc19-285f65f71c5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213931 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186213068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214213 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's recall what we learned in a previous lesson about linear equations with infinitely many solutions. \n\nSuppose we have the linear equation\n\n$$\n2x + 5 = 2x + 5.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n- the coefficient of $x$ on both sides of the equation is $2,$ and\n\n- the constant term on both sides of the equation is $5.$\n\nSince the coefficients of $x$ on both sides of the equation are equal, and the constants are also equal, it follows that this equation has *infinitely many solutions.* In fact, this equation is true for *any* value of $x.$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d.\n$$\n\nIf $a = c$ and $b = d$ are *both* true, then the equation is true for any value of $x.$\n\nThe goal of this lesson is to extend this idea to quadratic polynomials." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214213 #edb/ref 1065 13405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214213 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "93d3fd0c-7714-59b4-970d-29dc3c5d7545" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214213 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214223 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have two quadratic polynomials $P$ and $Q,$ given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Q(x) = dx^2 + ex + f.\n$$\n\nIf these polynomials are equal for *all* values of $x,$ we say that they're **identical.** This means their matching terms must be the same. In other words, we must have\n\n$$\na = d, \\qquad b = e, \\qquad c = f.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = x^2 + 5x + 4\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b,$ and $c$ are fixed numbers, and we're told that this equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\nSince this polynomial equation is true for all values of $x$, we can match the coefficients.\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a$, and on the right-hand side it is $1.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\na = 1.\n$$\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b$, and on the right-hand side it is $5.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nb = 5.\n$$\n\n- The constant on the left-hand side is $c$, and on the right-hand side it is $4.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nc = 4.\n$$\n\nThis process is known as **equating coefficients. **  It’s a useful tool whenever we know two polynomials are equal for all values of the variable.\n\nThis idea works for higher-degree polynomials too: when two polynomials are equal for all $x$, we can compare the coefficients of each power of $x$ and set them equal to each other." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214223 #edb/ref 1065 13433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214223 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ce7ce256-8fad-563a-81d9-48084c6f798e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214223 #edb/ref 1067 "Equating Coefficients of Quadratic Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214233 #edb/ref 1064 "We often use linear functions in real-world situations to describe relationships between quantities.\n\nFor example, suppose a writer is tracking their progress while typing a draft. They observe that\n\n- after $2$ minutes, they have typed $300$ words, and\n- after $5$ minutes, they have typed $750$ words.\n\nWe can build a linear function to model this situation.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of minutes, and $g(x)$ represent the number of words typed after $x$ minutes.\nThen, the linear function modeling this situation is\n\n$$\ng(x) = mx + b\n$$\n\nfor some slope $m$ and value $b$, which we need to find.\n\nFrom the two observations, we know two points on the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{1}, y_{1}) &= (2, 300) \\\\\n(x_{2}, y_{2}) &= (5, 750)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the given two points, we find the slope $m$ using the slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{750 - 300}{5 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{450}{3} \\\\\n&= 150\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the writer is typing at a rate of $150$ words per minute.\nSo, the linear function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= mx + b \\\\\n&= 150x + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $b$, we substitute one of the points, say $(2, 300)$, into this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n300 &= 150 \\cdot 2 + b \\\\\n300 &= 300 + b \\\\\nb &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means the starting number of words is $0$ - the writer began typing from scratch.\nTherefore, the linear equation that defines $g(x)$ is\n\n$$\ng(x) = 150x\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214233 #edb/ref 1065 13461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214233 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "75f58e28-fe24-58b2-857a-9c0b7070ccd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214233 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214234 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, when modeling real-life situations with linear functions, the independent variable does not start at zero.\n\nFor example, suppose a puzzle enthusiast is working on a large jigsaw puzzle. Their progress follows this pattern:\n\n- On the first day, they place $50$ pieces.\n- They add $30$ more pieces on each of the following days.\n\nWith this, we can construct a linear function $P$ for the total number of puzzle pieces placed after $d$ days, where\n\n$$\nd \\geq 1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's break down the pattern to understand it:\n\n- On day $d=1$, they place $P=50$ pieces.\n- On day $d=2$, they place $P=50+30=80$ pieces in total.\n- On day $d=3$, they place $P=50+30+30=110$ pieces in total.\n- $\\cdots$\n\nAnd so on.\n\nNotice that the amount added each day after the first is always $30$. So the total number of pieces placed includes\n\n- the initial $50$ pieces placed on the first day, and\n- $30$ pieces for each additional day *beyond the first*. Since the first $50$ pieces are placed on day $1$, the number of *additional* pieces placed by day $d$ equals\n$30(d - 1)$.\n\nTherefore, the total number of pieces $P$ placed after $d$ days, where\n\n$$\nd \\geq 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(d) &= 50 + 30(d - 1) \\\\\n&= 50 + 30d - 30 \\\\\n&= 30d + 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214234 #edb/ref 1065 13462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214234 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2f6ca0c8-4565-5090-8352-bc4db72075a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214234 #edb/ref 1067 "Linear Functions With Alternative Domains" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214235 #edb/ref 1064 "Rationalizing the denominator works similarly for algebraic expressions as it does for numerical ones. When the denominator contains two terms and includes radicals, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the *conjugate* of the denominator to eliminate the radical.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1+\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nTo find the conjugate of the denominator $1+\\sqrt{x}$, all we have to do is flip the sign between the two terms. So, the conjugate of the denominator is\n\n$$\n1\\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $1-\\sqrt{x}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{x}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{x}} \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{x}}{1 - \\sqrt{x}} &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{x})/((1 + \\sqrt{x})(1 - \\sqrt{x})) &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{x})/(1^{2} - (\\sqrt{x})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{x}}{1 - x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214235 #edb/ref 1065 13464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214235 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8dde46d5-669e-5c3a-b471-7541b1824862" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186214235 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232372 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232372 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232372 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232372 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232372 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d0a16f09-2e74-5931-9828-588ba4cfaa5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233067 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233067 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102613" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233067 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233067 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=3$ by computing the limit of a difference quotient." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233067 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d862336-2821-5236-aa0c-5f338ff1395d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233067 #edb/ref 1074 "*Method 1:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(3)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{10(3+h)^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{90+60h+10{h}^{2}-90}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{60h+10{h}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(60+10h) \\\\  & =60+(0) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Method 2:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10{x}^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10({x}^{2}-{3}^{2})}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}10(x+3) \\\\  & =10(3+3) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233094 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233094 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233094 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233094 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2d/2d6d392d4e0f773f962289075ffa07cd26353ebc00ec50e7473a5c992824417f.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the solid shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "552d9e5c-d038-53c1-b922-9832413afdda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233094 #edb/ref 1074 "The solid is a **right cylinder**. \n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The solid has two parallel circular bases. So, it's a cylinder.\n\n- The axis that passes through the centers of the circles is perpendicular to the bases. So, it's right." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233127 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233127 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233127 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233127 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9d/9d4a5a15c0fc8487aa827a275375c07f048f38ed76fd94b9c40223ff7c38fa9c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$, where $g(x)=f(x+a)+b.$ What are the values of $a$ and $b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233127 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d94abf8-99d4-59e5-a436-d04813696a03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233127 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that every point on the graph $y=g(x)$ is two units *to the right* and six units *up* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$ \n\nSo, we conclude that $g(x)=f(x-2)+6,$ and therefore $a=-2$ and $b=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233146 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233146 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233146 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233146 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the expression $81{m}^{4}-{n}^{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233146 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c434f49e-3ea2-550d-b853-d3c211a71434" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233146 #edb/ref 1074 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n81{m}^{4}-{n}^{4}={(9{m}^{2})}^{2}-{({n}^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{(9{m}^{2})}^{2}-{({n}^{2})}^{2}=(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(9{m}^{2}-{n}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares: \n\n$$\n9{m}^{2}-{n}^{2}={(3m)}^{2}-{n}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(9{m}^{2}-{n}^{2}) & =(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(3m+n)(3m-n)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233152 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233152 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233152 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233152 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=4$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+32?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233152 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b96d00b-58fa-5601-a22a-8b8182429232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233152 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x=4$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $0$ | $32$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $-8$ | $-32$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-4}={x}^{2}-2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}-2x-8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-4)(x+2)(x-4) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-4)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=4$ has multiplicity $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233268 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/79/791a76ebbb34751b2ce345243c5de4baf95a32357ce48830e3ea43043011b5e1.png)\n\nGiven the above sample space, find $P({X}^{′}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233268 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f9cab85-d551-5a63-8c24-8e21e5ba12ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233268 #edb/ref 1074 "The Venn diagram tells us that $P(X)=\\frac{2}{9}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}^{′}) & =1-P(X) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233276 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233276 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119805" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233276 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233276 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/95/95c30eab094a24beb6976262167401efa973dbac12781c69d074eb14e12e2743.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233276 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45f48cf6-e519-5407-abc6-4bbd59c8789a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233276 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we have to find $m+n.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n:$ \n\n![](images/87/87647211f77776c5f5f740c205fafb150eef1b9d3ebefb82df73a9fdcd953bdb.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$, we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=0.5=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $BH=2.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|m+n| & =AB \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.$ Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233293 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233293 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121632" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233293 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233293 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta +{62}^{∘})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233293 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9a8f3f4-0ef5-5e5b-92f7-76c0a9db8aab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233293 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that the cofunction of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and the angle complementary to $\\theta +{62}^{∘}$ is\n\n$$\n{90}^{∘}-(\\theta +{62}^{∘})={90}^{∘}-\\theta -{62}^{∘}={28}^{∘}-\\theta .\n$$\n\n So we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta +{62}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({90}^{∘}-(\\theta +{62}^{∘}))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({28}^{∘}-\\theta ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233302 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233302 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122326" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233302 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233302 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the diameter of a circle if its area is $\\frac{9}{25}\\pi ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233302 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd94009d-7285-58c6-9df1-2341d32131ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233302 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the area of a circle is $A=\\pi {r}^{2}$, where $r$ is the radius. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{25}\\pi  & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{25}\\cancel{\\pi } & =\\cancel{\\pi }{r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diameter is\n\n$$\nd=2\\cdot r=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233306 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233306 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233306 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233306 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233306 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55febbab-96d0-5d10-a73b-1109a9b31684" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233306 #edb/ref 1074 "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡0=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1+1=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233424 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233424 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233424 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233424 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following expressions are **irrational**?\n\nI. ${8}^{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}$\n\nII. ${3}^{20}\\cdot 7.0523$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {2}^{-15}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233424 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef24e72f-f752-5e87-a5ef-c5be48597b10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233424 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that: \n\n- the product of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Expression I is rational. Notice that $\\sqrt{36}=6$ is a rational number. The number ${8}^{5}$ is rational too. So, their product must be rational.\n\n- Expression II is rational. Since both ${3}^{20}$ and $7.0523$ are rational, their product must be rational.\n\n- Expression III is irrational. Since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational and ${2}^{-15}$ is rational, their product must be irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233432 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233432 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233432 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233432 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ce/cec25c6ab182494771824bae8c2182bf38b57e2d1e81d913e3e49916c8cc3d60.png)\n\nThe residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on a set of bivariate numerical data $(x,y).$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. When $x=8,$ the regression equation overestimates the value of $y.$\n\nII. The residuals appear to be randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\n\nIII. This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233432 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "510cace3-4d24-50e7-9ee2-e819b9c603a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233432 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $x=8$ is negative. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}<\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model overestimates  this data point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The plot shows the residuals tend to decrease as $x$ increases. So, there is a trend, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0bb186e7b1c1923e7e8b2776b202b1dcd9b930c56583199964e47530805d246d.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the residuals are *not* randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, our regression line is *not* a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233460 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233460 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233460 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233460 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following transformations in the order given:\n\n1\\. Stretch it by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n2\\. Stretch it by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n3\\. Translate it to the left by $10$ units\n\nWhat is the final function expression after applying all the transformations?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233460 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d424faa-a830-5c69-a1d8-32908ad96638" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233460 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the expression, let's apply each of the transformations in turn.\n\n1\\. Stretching the graph of $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis is equivalent to multiplying the function expression by $8.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(x).\n  $$\n\n2\\. Stretching the graph of $y=8f(x)$ by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $\\frac{1}{5}x.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(\\frac{1}{5}x)=8f(\\frac{x}{5}).\n  $$\n\n3\\. Translating the graph of $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5})$ by $10$ units to the left is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $(x+10).$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(\\frac{1}{5}(x+10))=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).\n  $$\n\nTherefore, the final function expression is $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233494 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233494 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233494 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233494 #edb/ref 1071 "If the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(7,-4)$ and $(a,b)$ is $(2,1),$\nthen $a+b=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233494 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd717350-e4af-57c0-a511-28912f1d1ece" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233494 #edb/ref 1074 "The given points are ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(2,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (2,1) & =(\\frac{7+a}{2},\\frac{-4+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=\\frac{7+a}{2} \\\\ 1=\\frac{-4+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}a=-3 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-3,6)$ and $a+b=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233504 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6-5x}{x+7}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09439e98-9680-5cbd-876f-1f24d85d22d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233504 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6-5x}{x+7}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{6-5y}{y+7}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{6-5y}{y+7} \\\\ x(y+7) & =6-5y \\\\ xy+7x & =6-5y \\\\ xy+5y & =6-7x \\\\ y(x+5) & =6-7x \\\\ y & =\\frac{6-7x}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{6-7x}{x+5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-16295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233542 #edb/ref 1071 "$(6-9\\,\\text{i})(3+7\\,\\text{i})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4508476-b89c-53c0-90e9-f3a9b08e0f33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233542 #edb/ref 1074 "Multiplying two complex numbers is similar to multiplying two binomials. We also use the fact that ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6-9\\,\\text{i})(3+7\\,\\text{i}) & =6\\cdot 3+6\\cdot 7\\,\\text{i}+(-9\\,\\text{i})\\cdot 3+(-9\\,\\text{i})\\cdot 7\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =18+42\\,\\text{i}-27\\,\\text{i}-63\\,{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =18+15\\,\\text{i}-63\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =18+15\\,\\text{i}+63 \\\\  & =81+15\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233592 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233592 #edb/ref 1069 "q-180277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233592 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233592 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a6/a61a6232b2041bab3d455a7d765311ddfae25f674f8dbbf79696d2137355b159.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram shown above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space.\n\nGiven that every outcome is equally likely, find $P({A}^{′}\\cup {B}^{′}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233592 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81a4737e-d96b-5774-b418-c2e889c0a2b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233592 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, ${A}^{′}\\cup {B}^{′}$ is represented by the area outside the circle $B$ or outside the circle $A$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/6a/6a3bb7b671e45aeb15d3239cae6fcedb7c36e52b15141d66b23e65066ab37edc.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in ${A}^{′}\\cup {B}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\n2+5+1=8\n$$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n2+9+5+1=17.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}^{′}\\cup {B}^{′})=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233616 #edb/ref 1069 "q-19821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233616 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233616 #edb/ref 1071 "Peter has $8$ shirts, $6$ pants, and $6$ pairs of shoes. If Peter wears one shirt, one pant, and one pair of shoes, how many different ways can Peter dress?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9db0cb85-c477-5f32-9094-207566837840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233616 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of outfits} & =\\text{Number of shirts} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of pants} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of pairs of shoes} \\\\  & =8\\times 6\\times 6 \\\\  & =288.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Peter can dress in $288$ different ways." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233682 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186232372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233682 #edb/ref 1069 "q-254742" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233682 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233682 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the frequency table below for a continuous variable $x.$ The total number of observations is $n=20,$ and the estimated sample mean is $\\overset{―}{x}≈13.5.$\n\n| Range | Frequency $({f}_{i})$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0\\le x<10$ | $8$ |\n| $10\\le x<20$ | $7$ |\n| $20\\le x<30$ | $5$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nUsing the midpoint of each class interval to approximate all observations within that class, estimate the standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{n},$ using $n$ as the denominator in the variance formula.\n\nRounded to one decimal place, ${\\sigma }_{n}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233682 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac5ee09a-446b-5918-96fe-4cc239c6a7e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233682 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, we can estimate the variance by treating all observations within each class as though they were equal to the midpoint of that class. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{n}^{2}≈\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{K}{f}_{i}\\cdot ({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${f}_{i}$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n\n- ${m}_{i}$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class,\n\n- $n$ is the total number of observations, and\n\n- $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the estimated mean, calculated using the class midpoints.\n\nTo find the variance, we add columns to our table for the midpoints of each interval, the squared deviations of the midpoints from the mean, and the products of the squared deviations and frequencies.\n\n| Range | ${f}_{i}$ | ${m}_{i}$ | ${({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}$ | ${f}_{i}{({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0\\le x<10$ | $8$ | $5$ | $72.25$ | $578$ |\n| $10\\le x<20$ | $7$ | $15$ | $2.25$ | $15.75$ |\n| $20\\le x<30$ | $5$ | $25$ | $132.25$ | $661.25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, we divide the sum of all the values in the last column by $20$ (the total number of observations):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{n}^{2} & ≈\\frac{578+15.75+661.25}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1255}{20} \\\\  & =62.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the estimated standard deviation is the square root of the estimated variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{n} & ≈\\sqrt{62.75}≈7.9\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233712 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233712 #edb/ref 1069 "q-27541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233712 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233712 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/41/41875c5ab1216b78760c1c2dddec6ffc31bfd57d2da27f4797b1190dbf4af709.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233712 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ad05a96-8f5a-585b-894f-27d371577458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233712 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Hence, $c=-3.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}-3 & =1 \\\\ k-3 & =1 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=4-(-3)=7.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
